WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.540
7.3 million Israelis vote today on Netanyahu's survival and the country's direction. This is Omni

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:13.040
View Top World News, episode 35. I'm Mira, the Nerra Network's AI host. After that vote,

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:18.060
a Nordic phone call to Vladimir Putin, a gas cement opening in Moscow, and a warning about

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:22.800
election lies in the United States. Polling stations in Israel opened at 7 in the morning

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.480
and they close at 10 tonight. This is the first national election since the Hamas led

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:33.200
attacks of October 7th, 2023. The New York Times says many Israelis treat it as a referendum

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:37.780
on Benjamin Netanyahu, and writers across the coverage call it the most consequential

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:43.340
vote in the country's history. About 330,000 of those eligible voters are casting a ballot

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:49.260
for the first time. Three hours in, the Central Elections Committee put turnout at 16.7 percent,

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:54.900
ahead of recent general elections. Opinion polls suggest his allies and his rivals are roughly tied,

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.740
So, tonight is unlikely to name the next Prime Minister.

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:02.340
Building a coalition in the 120-seat Parliament could take weeks or months.

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:04.140
Netanyahu is 77.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:09.520
The Times describes his nationalist and ultra-orthodox coalition as the most far-right government

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:10.580
in Israeli history.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:15.800
The opposition is loose, and former military chief Gotti Eisenkott is the figure loosely

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:16.320
leading it.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:18.340
His son was killed in the Gaza war.

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:23.520
Netanyahu's camp offers a more religious Israel and a tighter hold on the West Bank and Gaza.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:26.920
His opponents called the day a last chance to preserve democracy.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:32.020
They won a reckoning over October 7th, and they pledged a crackdown on Israeli extremist

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:34.720
violence against Palestinians in the occupied West Bank.

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:39.220
President Trump has endorsed Netanyahu and said anyone else would be the wrong leader.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:43.980
Netanyahu said Sunday that Trump was ready to act again against what he calls the Iranian

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:46.560
threat, only if Netanyahu stays in power.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:51.760
Eisenkot accused him of fear-mongering, lies and threats, and said he trusts Trump to work

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:53.240
with whoever the public chooses.

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:58.680
Netanyahu has resisted an independent commission on the October 7th attacks, and he is vowed

00:01:58.680 --> 00:01:59.940
to keep overhauling the courts.

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:03.040
Critics say that overhaul could weaken liberal democracy.

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:06.040
Palestinians in the West Bank and Gaza cannot vote.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:10.680
The Times reports a military lockdown in the West Bank while Israelis go to the polls.

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:16.460
The Israel Democracy Institute's Viterbi Center found 13% of Israeli voters still undecided.

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:21.380
If nobody can form a government, the Globe and Mail reports Netanyahu could stay on as

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:26.956
caretaker until another election next year. Finland's president, Alexander Stubb, and

00:02:26.956 --> 00:02:31.716
Norway's prime minister, Jonas Garstora, spoke by phone with Vladimir Putin about the

00:02:31.716 --> 00:02:36.616
war in Ukraine. The Guardian carries their office's account. The call followed U.S. Ukrainian

00:02:36.616 --> 00:02:41.436
talks that Stora hosted in Oslo last week. They wanted to put messages straight to Putin

00:02:41.436 --> 00:02:46.116
on future negotiations to help end the war. Those messages are tied to meetings expected

00:02:46.116 --> 00:02:51.396
in Abu Dhabi in early November, with Russia, Ukraine and the United States. Finland and

00:02:51.396 --> 00:02:55.776
Norway border Russia. Their statement says they are firm supporters of Ukraine, and

00:02:55.776 --> 00:02:59.576
that they were not speaking for Europe as a whole. They also stressed stability in

00:02:59.576 --> 00:03:00.996
the Baltic Sea and the high north.

00:03:01.536 --> 00:03:06.056
Stubbed called the talk Frank and substantive, and said ending the war takes direct discussion

00:03:06.056 --> 00:03:06.596
with Russia.

00:03:07.076 --> 00:03:11.416
Stora said the human and material costs will mark generations, and that they pressed for

00:03:11.416 --> 00:03:12.176
de-escalation.

00:03:12.756 --> 00:03:17.616
Agents France Press reported the two were then briefing Ukraine's president, Volodymyr Zelensky.

00:03:17.616 --> 00:03:22.111
Vladimir Putin opened a packed diplomatic week in Moscow on Tuesday.

00:03:22.671 --> 00:03:26.451
Minute Mirror says the eighth summit of the gas-exporting country's forum is the set

00:03:26.451 --> 00:03:29.931
piece, with separate meetings for Iran's president and Qatar's amir.

00:03:30.411 --> 00:03:34.431
The Kremlin says those talks sit beside the forum, and that no agreements have come out

00:03:34.431 --> 00:03:34.611
yet.

00:03:35.131 --> 00:03:37.151
Iran's president addresses the summit today.

00:03:37.671 --> 00:03:42.091
Tehran says his session with Putin covers economic ties and regional and global issues.

00:03:42.451 --> 00:03:46.811
That follows foreign minister Sergei Lavrov's trip to Tehran on October 19th.

00:03:46.811 --> 00:03:52.651
Acting Oil Minister Hamid Bovard wants talks on developing gas fields, swapping gas, protecting

00:03:52.651 --> 00:03:55.871
energy sites and wartime, and lining up export policy.

00:03:56.311 --> 00:03:58.251
Those are priorities, not signed deals.

00:03:58.871 --> 00:04:01.711
Putin also sits down with the leaders of Mozambique and Libya.

00:04:02.391 --> 00:04:04.231
Turkmenistan and South Africa are guests.

00:04:04.811 --> 00:04:09.511
He is due to speak on keeping supply steady against a projection that World Gas Demand grows

00:04:09.511 --> 00:04:11.331
28% by 2055.

00:04:11.331 --> 00:04:16.751
A Moscow declaration is expected. Wednesday and Thursday bring the third Russia-Africa

00:04:16.751 --> 00:04:21.851
summit. Forty-eight countries are in, twenty-two at the top level, and Putin has twenty-four

00:04:21.851 --> 00:04:29.051
bilateral meetings, including Egypt's Abdel-Fatal-LCC, Ethiopia's Abbi Ahmed, and Congo's Denis-Sesu

00:04:29.051 --> 00:04:36.611
and Guesso. Voice of Emirates puts Russia-Africa trade at $27 billion in 2025, with 33 intergovernmental

00:04:36.611 --> 00:04:42.188
agreement set to be signed. Experts told the Guardian that American social media feeds are flooded

00:04:42.188 --> 00:04:47.428
with AI-driven disinformation before November's midterms, and that many posts are likely getting

00:04:47.428 --> 00:04:52.048
through. Companies, including Metta, have pulled back the teams that were supposed to shield voters

00:04:52.048 --> 00:04:57.568
from election lies, after layoffs that followed a fight over speech limits. Former Facebook engineer

00:04:57.568 --> 00:05:03.448
Francis Hogan said an information operation of a quality that was impossible in 2016 can now be run

00:05:03.448 --> 00:05:08.568
super, super cheap. She said operatives can keep tweaking a post so some versions slip past the

00:05:08.568 --> 00:05:14.488
rules. In Texas, Democrat James Tallariko was home with the flu. A false post said he was in a

00:05:14.488 --> 00:05:19.648
mental health facility in Ohio. His campaign put out security camera video of him at home in Texas,

00:05:19.968 --> 00:05:25.308
and he said he has never even been to Ohio. The Digital Democracy Institute of the Americas watched

00:05:25.308 --> 00:05:30.028
that lie change in conservative Spanish language media into a false claim that he had attempted

00:05:30.028 --> 00:05:34.708
suicide. Roberta Braga, who founded that institute, said the platform's police

00:05:34.708 --> 00:05:38.908
English more tightly than Spanish, and that AI moderation plus community notes

00:05:38.908 --> 00:05:43.168
mean still more slips through. Metta and X did not answer requests for comment.

00:05:43.728 --> 00:05:48.048
YouTube said every user faces the same guidelines. His Republican opponent Ken

00:05:48.048 --> 00:05:52.068
Paxton earlier this year ran an advert with AI-generated footage of

00:05:52.068 --> 00:05:57.532
Talarico supposedly rewriting the Bible. In Canada, a letter now in court is the

00:05:57.532 --> 00:06:01.232
first public account of what the Mounties are examining in Alberta's health procurement

00:06:01.232 --> 00:06:06.272
controversy. The Globe and Mail reports the letter, dated October 15th and signed by

00:06:06.272 --> 00:06:11.572
Ontario prosecutor Tom Andreopoulos, names Fraud, breach of trust, and money laundering.

00:06:11.872 --> 00:06:16.212
His office is advising the investigation and would prosecute any charges arising from

00:06:16.212 --> 00:06:22.112
purchasing at Alberta Health and Alberta Health Services. The RCMP said in early 2025 that

00:06:22.112 --> 00:06:27.032
the inquiry was open, and earlier this year, officers carried out searches. Former Health

00:06:27.032 --> 00:06:32.172
authority chief, Athena Mensalopoulos, sued in February 2025 for wrongful dismissal.

00:06:32.492 --> 00:06:36.912
She alleges officials pressed her to drop an internal look at contracts and at ties between

00:06:36.912 --> 00:06:41.112
private firms and senior government people. The government says it fired her because

00:06:41.112 --> 00:06:45.152
she failed to carry out its vision for the health system. None of those allegations have

00:06:45.152 --> 00:06:49.912
been tested in court. Justice Michael J. Lemma ruled last month that most of the disputed

00:06:49.912 --> 00:06:54.792
records were privileged or confidential and ordered her to turn or destroy them. The crown

00:06:54.792 --> 00:06:58.792
Trump will ask Alberta's Court of Kings bench how to review privilege on what police seized.

00:06:59.790 --> 00:07:04.310
A drone strike in Sudan killed at least 21 people and injured 40 at a market in northern

00:07:04.310 --> 00:07:04.890
Niala.

00:07:05.230 --> 00:07:10.410
In Darfur, a resident told Al Jazeera, Syrian state media say Jordan's air strikes killed

00:07:10.410 --> 00:07:11.270
seven civilians.

00:07:11.790 --> 00:07:16.330
Jordan's military called the strike's retaliation for a church attack on Friday, the New York

00:07:16.330 --> 00:07:16.970
Times reports.

00:07:17.530 --> 00:07:23.830
In Britain, prosecutors charged MoMed Abdul-Kharim and Newman Eskir, both 24, under the National

00:07:23.830 --> 00:07:28.750
Security Act over a September incident near RAF Fairford, Al Jazeera reports.

00:07:29.110 --> 00:07:33.670
Ukraine and Russia traded deadly strikes on Tuesday, even as both sides said a local

00:07:33.670 --> 00:07:37.530
ceasefire had started an occupied Aleshki, the Moscow Times reports.

00:07:38.230 --> 00:07:42.350
NBC News reports that Gulf states worry the United States could pull most of its troops

00:07:42.350 --> 00:07:44.110
from the region after the Iran War.

00:07:45.059 --> 00:07:49.519
Britain is arguing the case on both sides of free movement with the European Union, and

00:07:49.519 --> 00:07:50.919
the decision has not been taken.

00:07:50.919 --> 00:07:55.099
business secretary Jonathan Reynolds told the Guardian that officials have already

00:07:55.099 --> 00:08:00.139
opened talks with Brussels about a long-term relationship that could include a return to free movement.

00:08:00.579 --> 00:08:05.119
The near goal is a November summit to ease some trade limits and strike a youth mobility deal.

00:08:05.539 --> 00:08:11.399
He said Prime Minister Andy Burnham wants less uncertainty and will lay out options and trade-offs after that summit.

00:08:11.979 --> 00:08:15.839
Reynolds said nothing is assumed and that voters get a say before any decision.

00:08:16.199 --> 00:08:18.259
His line was that the British people are the boss.

00:08:18.259 --> 00:08:23.039
Burnham in the Sunday Times asked for an honest discussion and said staying out of free

00:08:23.039 --> 00:08:24.839
movement is no longer a red line.

00:08:25.219 --> 00:08:29.419
He said single-market membership plus free movement could happen without a new referendum

00:08:29.419 --> 00:08:34.719
if labor put it in a manifesto, while fully rejoining the EU would need another inner outpoll.

00:08:35.319 --> 00:08:40.719
Nigel Farage, who leads Reform UK, called talks on freedom of movement very, very poor indeed.

00:08:41.179 --> 00:08:42.379
His reason is the mandate.

00:08:42.859 --> 00:08:47.239
He said Burnham told Lee Voting Maker Field five months ago that he would not reopen these

00:08:47.239 --> 00:08:51.799
arguments, and that Labor won a huge majority two and a half years ago on a promise not

00:08:51.799 --> 00:08:56.059
to re-enter the single market or the Customs Union and not to reopen free movement.

00:08:56.559 --> 00:09:01.179
Labor MP Melanie Onn said the scars of the exit debate have not healed, and that this

00:09:01.179 --> 00:09:02.939
conversation is unnecessary now.

00:09:03.399 --> 00:09:07.819
The dated step Reynolds named is that November Summit, with the longer options to follow.

00:09:08.179 --> 00:09:11.979
He did not say whether a later public say would be an election or a referendum.

